From 51b05a3c69a6041be2c1af0682300246e8ea018c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 22:35:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let functions track their named properties by shape

Statics on a constructor function were a 100% cache miss - 0 hits out of
200 000, sloppy, strict and class alike - and that is the idiom DeltaBlue reads
in its hot path, at 601x off, the worst throughput score in the suite. Two
things had to be fixed before the eligibility gate could be flipped, and
flipping it without them would have been a correctness bug rather than a no-op.

First, a function installed its own length, name and prototype with a bare
ownProperties.Put that never told the shape, and four constructors additionally
took a mutable ref to the property store through GetOwnProperties(), which
abandons the layout on the spot. Route every one through FastAddValue and drop
the four refs, which had become dead once their uses were converted. The shape's
key set is now the object's complete set of own named properties, which is what
GetPrototypeLookupShapeId and the transition entry require.

Second, every ordinary non-strict function carries the Annex B caller and
arguments as deferred cells from birth (P0-3), and a deferred cell abandoned
the shape - so without this, opting in would have helped strict functions and
classes only, and DeltaBlue's `Strength` is sloppy. Record such a key in the
shape with a NULL slot instead of abandoning it. The shape makes two claims and
only one needs the value: presence, which TryReadShapeSlot and
TryWriteShapeSlot use, and absence, which GetPrototypeLookupShapeId and
TryCreateShapeSlot use. A key present with a null slot keeps both true -
absence reasoning sees the key and declines, and all three fast paths already
reject a null slot or a descriptor whose value is not a JSValue, so a read or a
write falls through to the generic path that knows how to realize the cell. A
private name still abandons: it is per-class-evaluation, so admitting one would
mint a shape per instantiation instead of sharing a chain.

Then opt in, exact-type in both JSFunction and JSClass so no other subclass
inherits a claim it has not earned. All four function rows go from 0 / 200 000
to 199 999 / 1, and no site in the whole corpus abandons a shape any more.
Wall clock on a 10 M-iteration loop shaped like DeltaBlue's satisfy() - two
static reads and a static method call per iteration - five interleaved pairs
with only the overrides differing: median of paired ratios 0.905, every pair the
same direction.

Thirteen test cases in PropertyShapeCacheTests. The ones that matter are not the
hit rates but the Annex B surface: caller and arguments keeping their
non-writable, non-enumerable, non-configurable DATA descriptor, reading caller
while the function is on the stack, the Function.prototype poison pills still
throwing, and a null-slot key still reading through the generic path after its
site has been warmed. Plus a function's own length/name/prototype values,
attributes and enumeration order unchanged, redefining them, a bound function's
name and length, a static redefined as an accessor mid-loop, and delete
revealing an inherited static.

One pre-existing test changed with it. AnInheritedAccessorIsNotSlotCached
asserted zero cache hits for a script that also called Object.create - itself a
named read on a function object, which this makes cacheable, so it was quietly
supplying one hit. The prototype is now linked with __proto__ in the literal so
the script performs no other cacheable read and the exact assertion keeps its
teeth rather than being loosened.
